  "teamId": 140,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Texas Rangers were a team in transition, fielding a roster that was neither fully committed to contention nor fully committed to rebuilding, and the results reflected that uncomfortable middle ground. They finished 60-102, one of the worst records in the American League, surrendering 815 runs against a lineup that could only muster 625. Yet within that difficult season lived genuine moments of electricity, most of them supplied by a breakout star no one saw coming.\n\nAdolis García arrived in 2021 as a 28-year-old rookie who had spent years grinding through the minors and foreign leagues, and he announced himself immediately. His home runs in April set the tone for what would be his calling card all season — high-leverage, momentum-swinging blasts that gave Rangers fans something real to cheer about. His three-run shot on April 21 and his jaw-dropping walk-off-type homer on May 21, a clutch three-run blast worth nearly 0.8 WPA, were the brightest moments of the first half. He was, in a very real sense, the reason to watch.\n\nAs the calendar turned past the trade deadline and Joey Gallo was shipped to the Yankees, the Rangers leaned into youth and opportunism. Jonah Heim emerged as a quietly productive catcher, delivering clutch home runs in late July and early August. Andy Ibáñez chipped in timely hits, and journeymen like Jason Martin and Brock Holt produced their own fleeting moments of contribution. But the bullpen remained a persistent wound — Josh Sborz and Joe Barlow repeatedly found themselves on the wrong end of big swings, surrendering grand slams and late-game leads that turned potential wins into losses and defined the team's inability to close out games.\n\nSeptember offered a modest reprieve, with the Rangers posting a 12-15 record that was at least respectable compared to the August collapse. García added one final signature moment with a clutch double in late September, a reminder of what he meant to this franchise going forward. The 2021 Rangers were a hard team to watch but not a lifeless one — they had a genuine star in García, a promising catcher in Heim, and a front office signaling that better days were being built. The 102 losses stung, but the seeds of something better were quietly being planted.",
